Mirror of patch 0005 in the save direction: on wasm the traditional
QFileDialog::getSaveFileName can't produce a persistent writable
filesystem handle. Use the wasm-native QFileDialog::saveFileContent
which takes (bytes, suggested-name) and triggers a browser download.

Flow: db.saveAs() into a MEMFS scratch path, read the resulting bytes,
hand them to saveFileContent. Works uniformly for both in-memory and
file-backed databases because db.saveAs() serialises the current state
regardless of source.

--- a/src/MainWindow.cpp
+++ b/src/MainWindow.cpp
@@ -710,6 +710,32 @@ void MainWindow::fileSave()

 bool MainWindow::fileSaveAs() {
+#ifdef __EMSCRIPTEN__
+    // On wasm we can't return the user a writable file handle, so serialise
+    // the current db into MEMFS and hand the bytes to the browser as a
+    // download via QFileDialog::saveFileContent().
+    const QString scratch = QStringLiteral("/tmp/wasm-save/db.sqlite");
+    QDir().mkpath(QStringLiteral("/tmp/wasm-save"));
+    QFile::remove(scratch);
+
+    if (!db.saveAs(scratch.toStdString())) {
+        QMessageBox::warning(this, QApplication::applicationName(),
+            tr("Error while serialising the database for download."));
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    QFile in(scratch);
+    if (!in.open(QIODevice::ReadOnly)) {
+        QMessageBox::warning(this, QApplication::applicationName(),
+            tr("Error while reading serialised database bytes."));
+        return false;
+    }
+    const QByteArray bytes = in.readAll();
+    in.close();
+
+    const QString suggested = QFileInfo(db.currentFile()).fileName();
+    QFileDialog::saveFileContent(bytes, suggested.isEmpty() ? QStringLiteral("database.sqlite") : suggested);
+    return true;
+#endif

